<h2>The statutory framework is unchanged by geography</h2>
<p>Three routes to maintenance operate regardless of where the respondent earns. Section 144 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 (formerly Section 125 CrPC) provides the summary remedy for a wife, children and parents unable to maintain themselves against a person with sufficient means who neglects or refuses to maintain them. Section 24 of the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955 provides maintenance pendente lite and litigation expenses in matrimonial proceedings. Section 20 of the Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005 provides monetary relief within DV proceedings. "Sufficient means" has never meant means located in India; a respondent drawing a salary abroad is squarely within all three provisions.</p>

<h2>The affidavit-of-assets discipline</h2>
<p>In Delhi, maintenance proceedings are anchored by detailed affidavits of assets, income and expenditure which both parties must file. Courts have required full disclosure in a structured format covering income from every source, movable and immovable assets, bank accounts, liabilities and monthly expenditure. For a respondent employed abroad, the affidavit must grapple with the foreign employment specifically: employer, designation, gross and net salary in the foreign currency, housing and other allowances, tax and social-security deductions, and remittances to India. An affidavit that discloses only Indian bank balances while remaining silent on foreign employment is incomplete on its face, and the applicant should say so in a short, pointed response rather than a sprawling rejoinder.</p>

<h2>Proving the foreign income</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h4>Employment documents</h4><p>Offer letters, employment contracts and salary revision letters state the package authoritatively, including allowances that payslips sometimes split out. Visa category can corroborate minimum salary thresholds in some jurisdictions.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Payslips and tax filings</h4><p>Recent payslips and foreign tax returns show actual gross and net figures and mandatory deductions — the raw material for a disposable-income calculation rather than a headline number.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Bank and remittance records</h4><p>Foreign account statements and remittance records to Indian accounts show what the respondent actually has and sends. Regular remittances to his family are telling evidence of capacity.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Public and employer material</h4><p>Professional networking profiles, employer letters and published pay data for the role and city are legitimate secondary material, particularly where the respondent withholds primary documents.</p></div>
</div>
<p>Where the respondent participates but discloses selectively, interrogatories and directions to produce specific documents — payslips for named months, the employment contract, foreign tax filings — keep the record concrete. Where he does not participate at all, the applicant's documentary mosaic plus his lifestyle becomes the basis for estimation.</p>

<h2>Currency conversion and the cost-of-living adjustment</h2>
<p>The single most litigated point of principle in these cases is arithmetic. A gross foreign salary multiplied by the exchange rate produces a rupee figure that overstates capacity, because it ignores what it costs to earn that salary where it is earned.</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><h4>1. Establish gross foreign income</h4><p>From contracts, payslips and tax filings, in the foreign currency, including allowances.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h4>2. Deduct mandatory outgoings abroad</h4><p>Income tax, social-security contributions and comparable compulsory deductions in the country of employment.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h4>3. Account for genuine living costs abroad</h4><p>Reasonable rent, transport and subsistence in the foreign city — claimed figures tested against the documents, not accepted on assertion.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h4>4. Assess the applicant's needs in Indian terms</h4><p>Housing, household expenses, children's education and medical needs in Delhi, measured against the standard of living the family enjoyed.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h4>5. Fix a figure that balances both</h4><p>Maintenance reflects the respondent's real disposable capacity and the applicant’s real needs, not a mechanical percentage of a converted gross salary.</p></div>
</div>
<p>Both sides should assist this exercise rather than fight it. An applicant who concedes the respondent's documented rent abroad gains credibility for her own expense claims; a respondent who inflates his foreign expenses to a level his own bank statements contradict loses it.</p>

<h2>Standard of living as calibrating evidence</h2>
<p>Quantum is not fixed at subsistence. The standard of living during the marriage is a recognised factor, and in NRI cases it is often richly documented: international travel, the children's school abroad or in a premium Indian school, the apartment the couple occupied overseas, vehicles, and property acquired abroad or in India. This material serves two purposes — it calibrates the applicant's reasonable needs, and it independently corroborates income where the respondent pleads poverty inconsistent with the life the family demonstrably led.</p>

<h2>Adverse inference against concealment</h2>
<div class="note"><p>A respondent abroad who files no affidavit, files a bare one, or refuses to produce payslips and tax records does not thereby cap his liability at what he admits. Courts may draw an adverse inference from non-disclosure and estimate income from qualifications, employment history, lifestyle and the available material — and the estimate in such cases tends to resolve doubts against the party who created them.</p></div>
<p>For respondents, the practical lesson is the mirror image: candid disclosure with documents, including honest foreign outgoings, is the only route to a quantum fixed on real numbers rather than on inference.</p>

<h2>A note on what this article does not cover</h2>
<p>Fixing quantum is one question; recovering it from a respondent whose income and assets sit outside India is another, with its own machinery — that enforcement dimension is dealt with in a separate article on this site. The point of the present discussion is narrower: the quantum stage is where the foreign-income record is built, and a carefully proved disposable-income picture at this stage makes every later stage, including enforcement, easier.</p>
